---
<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/foundational/structures-loads-and-forces">

  <h2>Where you are, and what you will learn</h2>
  <p>
    This is a foundational lesson of Design, and it assumes nothing beyond everyday experience:
    you have sat on chairs, leaned on shelves and seen scaffolding around a building. No physics
    course and no workshop time is needed — every term is defined the moment it appears. The
    lesson follows the Cambridge IGCSE Design &amp; Technology syllabus's structures content:
    what structures are, what loads them, which forces tear at them, and how designers make
    them survive.
  </p>
  <p>By the end you will be able to:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>classify structures as shell, frame or mass, with natural and manufactured examples of each;</li>
    <li>distinguish static loads from dynamic loads, and say why the dynamic ones are harder to design for;</li>
    <li>name the five forces — tension, compression, shear, bending, torsion — and identify which is acting in a given situation;</li>
    <li>compute basic load figures: weight from mass, reactions at supports, and the bending moment of a simple beam;</li>
    <li>explain how triangulation and corrugation reinforce a structure, and why each works.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The habit to carry through the lesson: a structure is a <em>load path</em> — every force
    that enters it must travel through material, stage by stage, to the ground. Design is
    choosing that path.
  </p>

  <h2>The idea: every structure is a path for forces</h2>
  <p>
    Put a book on a table. Gravity pulls the book down; the book presses on the tabletop; the
    tabletop presses on the legs; the legs press on the floor; the floor presses on the
    building's frame, and so on down to the earth. Nothing in that chain is static in the
    force sense — every piece is squeezed or stretched, working silently to redirect one
    force (the book's weight) to its destination (the ground). That is what a structure
    <em>is</em>: a path that carries loads. Designers and engineers say a structure
    <strong>stands</strong> when every part of the path stays within what its material can
    take, and it <strong>fails</strong> when some link exceeds it — the shelf sags, the chair
    leg snaps, the bridge drops. Everything in this lesson is a way of answering two
    questions: what forces will travel this path, and will each member survive them?
  </p>

  <h2>Shell, frame and mass structures</h2>
  <p>
    Structures fall into three families, and the classification works on natural and
    manufactured examples alike. A <strong>shell structure</strong> holds its shape and
    carries load through a thin, curved outer skin: an egg, a car body, a drinks can, a
    soap bubble, a snail's shell — and, manufactured on purpose, an aircraft fuselage or a
    bike helmet. The curve is the trick: a sheet that folds flat is weak, but the same
    material curved into a shell spreads any push over its whole surface, which is why you
    cannot crush an egg end-to-end in one hand and why an empty can dents the moment its
    curve is cut. A <strong>frame structure</strong> carries load through a skeleton of
    separate members: a bicycle frame, a scaffolding tower, a crane, a tent, an electricity
    pylon — and in nature, a spider's web or the bones of your hand. Frame members are
    individual struts and ties, each doing one job, and the design questions are about the
    joints and the geometry (triangulation, coming later). A <strong>mass structure</strong>
    resists loads by sheer bulk and weight: a dam, a sandbag wall, a brick garden wall, a
    mountain, an ice shelf. Nothing is hollow or clever about it — gravity holds it down and
    its own weight makes it expensive to push over. Mass structures are heavy, so they are
    used where weight is free (against water, which is heavy too) and avoided where weight
    costs energy (bicycles, aircraft).
  </p>
  <Widget no="10" caption="shell, frame or mass? file each structure" widget="sort-buckets" config={slFamilySort} />
  <p>
    The same object can mix families: a house is a mass structure at its foundation, a frame
    in its timber skeleton and a shell in its tiled roof. Classification is a lens, not a
    box.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "strong material makes a strong structure, so steel is
  always best." It seems reasonable because steel is the strongest common material and we
  associate it with bridges and towers. But the paper-bridge schoolroom classic proves the
  opposite: a single sheet of paper lies limp across a 15 cm gap, yet the same sheet folded
  into pleats carries a stack of coins — same material, new geometry. Materials supply
  strength; <em>shape decides how that strength is used</em>. The correct picture: an eggshell
  (calcium carbonate, a brittle chalk-like ceramic) outperforms a flat steel plate of the same
  weight across the same span, because the shell's curve turns pushing into membrane
  tension. Choose material after shape, never instead of it.</p>
  <Widget no="9" caption="flat vs pleated — drag the divider" widget="compare" config={slPaperCompare} />

  <h2>Static and dynamic loads</h2>
  <p>
    A <strong>load</strong> is any force a structure must carry. <strong>Static loads</strong>
    are steady and unchanging: the weight of the structure itself (the <em>dead load</em> —
    roof tiles, walls, the shelf board), the things it permanently carries, and the slow,
    predictable weights like furniture and a still tank of water. Static loads are the easy
    case: you can weigh them once and design for them. <strong>Dynamic loads</strong> move,
    hit or shift: a person dropping onto a chair, wind gusting against a sign, a truck
    crossing a bridge, a door slamming, an earthquake. Dynamic loads are harder for two
    reasons. First, <em>impact multiplies force</em>: landing stiff-legged from a 30 cm step
    can load your knees with several times your body weight for the instant of deceleration —
    a designer cannot simply read the number off a scale. Second, dynamic loads
    <em>repeat</em>: a member that survives one gust may fail after a million of them, the
    fatigue problem that grounds aircraft parts and cracks old bridges. Designers answer
    dynamic loads with safety factors (design the shelf for five times the books), with
    damping and flexibility (a tent flexes in wind where a brick wall would crack), and by
    testing to destruction. One clarifying example with numbers: a 60 kg person
    <em>standing</em> on a platform loads it with about 590 N (weight = mass times gravity,
    60 × 9.81 ≈ 590 N); the same person <em>jumping</em> onto it can momentarily load it with
    2,000 N or more — a dynamic load four times the static one, arriving in a tenth of a
    second.
  </p>
  <Widget no="8" caption="call the static load" widget="estimate" config={slStandEst} />
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "a static load and a dynamic load of the same size stress
  a structure the same way." It seems reasonable because a force is a force — 500 N is 500 N.
  But structure failure depends on time as well as size: the dynamic 500 N arrives with
  momentum that must be absorbed, it can excite vibration (a bridge swaying at its own
  natural frequency), and repeated cycles grow microscopic cracks into fatal ones. The
  correct picture: static design asks "can it hold this once?", dynamic design asks "can it
  hold this arriving fast, moving, and a hundred thousand times?" The London Millennium
  Bridge opened in 2000 and wobbled shut within days — not from overload, but from
  pedestrians' synchronised footsteps feeding a dynamic load the designers had modelled as
  static sideways pushes.</p>
  <Widget no="7" caption="bet before the reveal — what wobbled the bridge?" widget="predict" config={slMillenniumPredict} />

  <h2>The five forces that attack every structure</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Tension</strong> pulls a member apart: the cable of a crane hook, the string of a
    drawn bow, the ties of a suspension bridge, your shoelaces. <strong>Compression</strong>
    pushes it together: table legs, brick walls, the stone columns of a temple, the struts of
    a bicycle frame. Both are the gentle pair — a material in pure tension or pure compression
    uses its full strength efficiently. <strong>Shear</strong> slices across a member, two
    forces sliding past each other in opposite directions: scissors cutting paper, a paper
    punch, the rivet joining two overlapping plates pulled in opposite directions, and the
    failure of a poorly glued joint that slides apart. <strong>Bending</strong> is tension
    and compression sharing one member: a shelf loaded with books stretches its bottom
    surface while its top surface squeezes, with a neutral middle doing little. Bending is
    expensive — most of the material near the middle is barely working, which is why
    designers remove it (the I-beam's web, the hollow tube) or add material only at the
    faces. <strong>Torsion</strong> twists a member around its axis: a screwdriver shaft
    under load, a wrung-out towel, a car's drive shaft, a spindle turning a blind.
  </p>
  <Widget no="6" caption="pair each force with its everyday case" widget="match" config={slForceMatch} />
  <p>
    Numbers make it real. Tension: a sign of mass 12 kg hangs from a steel wire, so the wire
    carries a force of 12 × 9.81 ≈ 118 N — about 120 N, throughout its whole length. Torsion:
    a screwdriver blade 30 mm across transmits a hand force of 20 N at its 15 mm radius, a
    twisting moment of 20 × 0.015 = 0.3 N·m — small, but a narrow blade concentrates it.
    Bending: the worked beam below turns a load and a span into the moment the shelf or beam
    must survive.
  </p>
  <Widget no="5" caption="find the faulty line — forces with numbers" widget="spot-error" config={slWireSpot} />
  <Widget no="1" caption="one load, two lives — an angled force and its horizontal and vertical components" widget="diagramBuilder" config={loadCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> drag the arrow's head and read the three numbers. The
    arrow is one force — but any force can be replaced by two components at right angles, and
    the dashed lines show them. Start at 8 N horizontal and 6 N vertical and the magnitude
    reads 10 N: the three sides of a right-angled triangle. This is the daily arithmetic of
    structures — a sign pulled by an angled cable loads its wall fixings with both the
    vertical pull (which the bracket carries in shear) and the horizontal tug (which tries to
    pull the fixing out). One force, entering the structure; two members, carrying it away.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "bending and shear are the same thing because both
  involve a member 'giving way' across its length." It seems reasonable because both fail a
  beam sideways. But they are different force systems: shear is two equal forces sliding
  past each other across a cut (scissors through paper), while bending is a gradient —
  tension growing down one face, compression growing down the other. The correct picture:
  the two stresses even have different failure surfaces — a shear cut is clean and flat, a
  bending break is jagged and starts at the tension face, which is why a broken shelf shows
  its torn underside. Test it: paper resists bending poorly but resists shear barely at
  all — it cuts with almost no force, yet the same paper pleated into corrugations becomes
  stiff enough to bend back.</p>

  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> static loads sit still and dynamic ones move, and five forces —
    compression, tension, bending, shear, torsion — attack every structure made, whichever
    family it belongs to.
  </p>

  <h2>Beams: the worked example that ties the forces together</h2>
  <p>
    A shelf between two brackets is a <strong>beam</strong> — the most common structural
    member in design. Take a shelf of span 1.2 m with a 300 N load (about 30.6 kg of
    dictionaries) sitting at the middle. The two supports share the load equally, 150 N each;
    the shelf's middle experiences a bending moment of W × L ÷ 4 = 300 × 1.2 ÷ 4 = 90 N·m —
    the number that decides whether the shelf sags visibly or stays straight. Change the
    numbers below and watch which quantity responds to what.
  </p>
  <Widget no="2" caption="the shelf formula — load W on the middle of a span L" widget="sliderLab" config={beamCfg} />
  <p>
    <strong>What to notice:</strong> the support reactions track the load only — double the
    books and each bracket carries double. The bending moment tracks load <em>and</em> span:
    doubling the span to 2.4 m at the same 300 N doubles the moment to 180 N·m, which is why
    long unsupported shelves sag while short ones do not, and why real shelving calls for a
    bracket at every 60 cm or so rather than trusting the material. Structure is proportional
    thinking: the formula W·L/4 is small, but it is the same one a bridge engineer uses at
    full scale.
  </p>
  <Widget no="4" caption="place the bracket spacing" widget="place" config={slBracketPlace} />

  <h2>Reinforcement: triangulation and corrugation</h2>
  <p>
    Two reinforcement strategies cover most of design. <strong>Triangulation</strong> fixes
    the geometry problem of frames: four members joined into a rectangle can lean into a
    parallelogram without any member changing length — the garden gate that sags, the
    scaffolding that sways. Add one diagonal and the rectangle becomes two triangles, and a
    triangle cannot deform without one of its members stretching or squashing — which members
    are built to resist. The whole of structural engineering hides in that observation: the
    bicycle frame, the electricity pylon, the roof truss, the crane jib and the Eiffel Tower
    are all assemblies of triangles, with every diagonal either pulling (a tie, in tension)
    or pushing (a strut, in compression). Corrugation fixes the same problem for sheets: a
    flat sheet bends easily, but fold it into a wave — the roof sheet, the cardboard box's
    inner flute, the pleated paper — and the folds act like a series of tiny I-beams, moving
    material away from the neutral middle to the faces where bending stress lives. Corrugated
    cardboard earns its strength this way: the fluted middle layer holds the two flat liners
    apart, converting a sandwich of weak paper into a stiff panel that ships the world's
    goods.
  </p>
  <Widget no="3" caption="true or false — reinforcement by geometry" widget="judge" config={slTriangJudge} />
  <p>
    <strong>So far:</strong> structures are load paths in three families (shell, frame,
    mass), loaded statically or dynamically, attacked by five forces, and defended by
    geometry — triangles to lock frames, folds to stiffen sheets. One more layer of design
    judgement remains: reading real objects and asking which force is where.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> "reinforcement means adding more material — a thicker
  board, a heavier bracket." It seems reasonable because more stuff feels stronger. But
  reinforcement is mostly about <em>placement</em>, not amount: the diagonal of a
  triangulated gate might be 2% of the gate's mass and still eliminate the sag, because it
  changes the geometry that lets the sag happen; corrugation stiffens a sheet by moving
  material, adding none. The correct picture: ask where the force wants to go and put
  material on that path — an I-beam has less material than the solid block it replaces and
  is barely less stiff. Contrasting example: bicycle makers slim the tubes and add no
  weight, because a round tube is already the corrugation idea in rotation — the same
  stiffness as a solid rod at a fraction of the mass.</p>

  <h2>Check yourself</h2>
  <p>Try these before moving on. Feedback follows each answer.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>An egg, a scaffolding tower and a dam: classify each as shell, frame or mass, and give one natural example of the family the other two belong to.</li>
    <li>A shelf of span 1.6 m carries a 400 N load at its middle. What does each support carry, and what is the maximum bending moment?</li>
    <li>A garden gate sags into a parallelogram. Why does adding one diagonal member fix it — and what forces does that diagonal now carry?</li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    <strong>Feedback:</strong> (1) Egg — shell; scaffolding tower — frame; dam — mass. Natural
    examples: shell — an egg, a nut, a snail's shell; frame — a spider's web, a skeleton,
    a tree branch network; mass — a sand dune, a coral head, a boulder. (2) Each support
    carries 400 ÷ 2 = 200 N; the maximum bending moment is W·L/4 = 400 × 1.6 ÷ 4 = 160 N·m.
    If you divided by 2 instead of 4 for the moment, you computed the reaction twice — the
    moment lives in the middle, where the lever arms meet. (3) The rectangle could deform
    into a parallelogram with no member changing length; the diagonal converts it into two
    triangles, which cannot deform without stretching or squashing a member. The diagonal
    becomes either a tie in tension (if it crosses with the corner dropping) or a strut in
    compression, depending on the load direction — which is why both ties and struts exist
    in every truss.
  </p>

  <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>A structure is a load path: every entering force must travel through material to the ground, and failure is a path that breaks.</li>
    <li>Shell structures carry load through curved skins (egg, can, car body); frames through member skeletons (pylon, bike, tent); mass structures through sheer weight (dam, garden wall).</li>
    <li>Static loads are steady and designable; dynamic loads arrive fast, repeat, and multiply through impact — design for multiples, not the number on the scale.</li>
    <li>The five forces: tension pulls, compression pushes, shear slides across a cut, bending stretches one face while squeezing the other, torsion twists.</li>
    <li>Bending is the expensive one — remove the idle middle (I-beams, tubes) or reinforce the faces.</li>
    <li>A beam's midspan moment is W·L/4: load times span, quartered — reactions share the load, the moment scales with span too.</li>
    <li>Triangulation locks frames; corrugation stiffens sheets — reinforcement is geometry and placement, not more material.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Where this goes next</h2>
  <p>
    The sibling foundational lesson <em>Design Is Deciding</em> steps back from structures to
    the discipline as a whole — the decisions behind every product, from hierarchy and
    affordance to anthropometrics and sustainability. The intermediate lessons then cover the
    communication and digital side of the subject — typography, grids, prototyping and user
    research — while the load-path thinking you built here returns in any later work on
    materials and making.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Why is a tube stiffer than a solid rod of the same weight, and an I-beam stiffer than the
    block it replaces? The answer is the second moment of area, and it is the deepest idea in
    this lesson. Bending stress in a member is proportional to its distance from the neutral
    axis — the middle layer that neither stretches nor squeezes. Double the depth of a
    rectangular beam and its stiffness against bending rises not by 2 but by 8 (stiffness
    scales with depth cubed), because the outer fibres now work at double the lever arm while
    carrying the same forces over a bigger cross-section of them. This single scaling law
    explains the entire family of efficient sections: the I-beam concentrates material in two
    flanges at maximum depth and leaves a thin web to fight shear; the tube is the same idea
    wrapped around an axis, equally strong in every bending direction and also good against
    torsion (a twist is just bending at an angle around the axis, resisted by material far
    from the centre); the corrugated sheet is the I-beam repeated cheaply; the bamboo culm is
    the tube grown naturally, hollow and ringed. When a designer "removes the idle middle",
    they are applying d³ scaling with a pencil — which is why the strongest structures are
    always the ones that look like they have holes in the right places.
  </p>
  <p>
    The second deep idea is that structures fail by their weakest link in a specific force
    system, so good designers name the failure mode before choosing the fix. A sagging gate
    fails geometrically (no triangulation); a snapped shelf fails in bending (add depth or
    support); a sheared rivet fails in shear (bigger diameter, or two rivets to share the
    load); a wrung snapped screwdriver tip fails in torsion (wider blade); a dented can fails
    as a shell (a full can resists where an empty one crumples — the liquid supports the
    skin). Diagnosis before remedy is the professional habit: add the right material to the
    right member for the right force, and most "strength problems" turn out to be geometry
    problems wearing a disguise.
  </p>

